{"step_index":43,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-07T07:37:00Z","input_tokens":4336,"cache_read_tokens":74736,"output_tokens":22169,"content":"Now the mission content: 15 missions (5 per level) as structured JSON, as FR-MISI-12 asks.","thinking":"Now I need to design the actual mission JSON content, making sure each mission's solution is correct and verifiable. I'm laying out grids as rows of characters and defining the shared action palette for the grid-based missions: forward, turn left, turn right, collect, water, and jump.\n\nFor Level 1 (basic sequencing, no repeat blocks), I'm sketching mission 1 as a water-cycle themed grid puzzle where a drop moves upward through three forward steps to reach a cloud goal, with a short two-sentence story. Mission 2 is a card-sequencing activity about the water cycle, where the player orders cards showing evaporation, condensation, rain, and runoff into the correct causal sequence.\n\nFor mission 3, I'm designing a grid puzzle themed around a seedling needing sunlight, where the player collects a sun item before navigating turns to reach the goal pot, mapping out the start position, facing direction, and forward/turn sequence needed to collect the item and finish the path.\n\nFor mission 4, I'm starting the sequencing cards for \"Plant Growth Stages\" - seed planted, seed sprouts, growing leaves - building out the ordered card set with matching icons.\n\nThen for mission 5, I'm designing the column addition sequence for 27+15, breaking it into steps: writing the numbers stacked, adding the ones column to get 12, carrying the 1 and writing 2, then adding the tens column.\n\nI'm also checking the overall level structure, considering whether to add a sixth challenge to Level 1, but deciding to stick with five sequences as the minimum works fine.\n\nNow moving to Level 2, which introduces looping with repeat blocks enabled. For L2-M1, \"Perkalian Katak,\" the frog hero jumps on a numberline from 0 to 12 by repeating \"jump +3\" four times, teaching that 4×3 means repeating \"add 3\" four times -- the optimal solution uses just two blocks (a repeat-4 loop containing one add-3 action) versus four blocks without looping.\n\nFor L2-M2, \"Siram 5 Pot,\" I'm designing a grid where a girl character walks forward watering pots in a row, solved optimally with a repeat-5 loop containing forward and water actions, using legend symbols to show watered versus unwatered flower pots.\n\nI'm starting to sketch L2-M3, a numberline mission about number patterns.\n\nFor number pattern missions, I'm working out that jumping by 5s from start to target 35 can be solved with a repeat-6 loop adding 5 each time, or alternatively a repeat-3 loop adding 10, keeping the palette simple with add:5 and add:2 blocks and marking checkpoints at 5, 10, 15 along a 0-50 numberline. I'm now sketching out L2-M4, a staircase-style grid mission themed around moon phases for the science track, starting the character at the bottom-left of a 5x5 grid.\n\nI'm tracing the diagonal path a repeating forward-turnLeft-forward-collect-turnRight pattern creates, confirming it collects four moon-phase items stepping diagonally up the grid, and now figuring out the most efficient block combination using a single repeat loop to replicate that motion.\n\nNext I'm sketching a new level, \"Keliling Kebun,\" with a nested-loop perimeter walk on a 4x4 grid — an outer repeat of 4 turns paired with an inner repeat of 3 forward-and-collect steps to gather flowers along the garden's edge.\n\nI'm tracing through the loop logic again to confirm: starting at (0,0), the repeated forward-and-collect pattern walks the perimeter clockwise and picks up all 12 items, ending back at the start cell where an item also sits. The grid layout with flowers on the border and trees in the center checks out, and I'm thinking of a short Indonesian story line about picking flowers around the garden with a pattern within a pattern.\n\nMoving to Level 2's subject mapping, M1 and M3 are Matematika while M2, M4, and M5 lean IPA — except M5 on the perimeter of a square is actually Matematika too, so I'm adjusting that. M4 covers moon phases and M2 covers plants, both IPA.\n\nFor Level 3, I'm designing the first conditional sorting puzzle: \"Genap atau Ganjil\" under Matematika, with items like 4, 7, 10, 3, 8, 15, 6 to sort into a blue bin for even numbers and a red bin for odd, plus a distractor condition checking for numbers greater than 10.\n\nI'm now drafting a second Level 3 puzzle under IPA, \"Terapung atau Tenggelam,\" sorting objects like wood, rock, a leaf, a nail, a rubber duck, and a metal spoon by whether they float or sink, and considering what distractor condition to pair with the floating property.\n\nThen I'm moving to a third Level 3 puzzle, \"Makanan Hewan,\" sorting animals like cows, tigers, rabbits, lions, and goats into herbivores versus carnivores.\n\nI realize all three sorting missions feel too similar in structure, so I'm reconsidering L3-M3 to instead be a math puzzle with nested conditions, sorting numbers into bins based on criteria like multiples of 5.\n\nLet me restructure the mission order instead: M1 for even/odd sorting (Math), M2 for floating objects (Science), M3 and M4 as grid-based repeat+if missions with themes like hopping stones or harvesting fruit (Science), and M5 as the nested sorting challenge with three number bins (Math). The herbivore classification idea can be dropped since this lineup covers the needed variety better.\n\nFor M3's \"jump over rocks\" grid, I'm testing whether a single repeat+if program—checking for a rock ahead and jumping over it, otherwise moving forward—works across different rock placements on a 7-wide path, since jump covers two spaces while forward covers one, meaning the iteration count shifts depending on rock positions.\n\nChecking layout C confirms 4 iterations too, as long as rocks aren't placed adjacently so a jump never lands on another rock. That means a simple repeated \"if rock ahead, jump, else forward\" loop of 4 blocks works across all three layouts, so I just need the UI to show which of the 3 boards is active, maybe with a small indicator that cycles during the run.\n\nFor the next level, \"Panen Buah,\" I'm setting up a 6x3 grid where the hero starts at the edge and moves through a row mixing ripe fruit and seedlings, needing to pick ripe ones and water seedlings. The solution loop repeats 5 times with a conditional check for ripe fruit versus seedling, and I'm working out three different layout variants plus how to represent the \"picked\" and \"watered\" states visually after the action.\n\nFor \"Tiga Kotak Bilangan,\" I'm sorting numbers into colored boxes based on two properties — multiples of 5 and even numbers — and I'm working through the priority order: multiples of 5 go yellow first, then remaining evens go blue, and everything else goes red.\n\nI'm also planning story prompts to keep them short, drafting two hints per tier for each mission, and now starting to note the optimal block counts needed to solve each level.\n\nNow I'll structure the solutions as JSON with id references, and plan to store each level's missions as separate JSON files under the content directory, validated with a zod schema in the loader. Starting to write those JSON files now.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"L1-M1\",\n    \"level\": 1,\n    \"order\": 1,\n    \"title\": \"Tetes Air Naik ke Awan\",\n    \"subject\": \"IPA\",\n    \"concept\": \"sequencing\",\n    \"story\": \"Matahari memanaskan laut, Tetes Air jadi ringan sekali. Bantu ia naik ke awan!\",\n    \"goal\": \"Bawa 💧 sampai ke ☁️.\",\n    \"world\": {\n      \"type\": \"grid\",\n      \"width\": 3,\n      \"height\": 5,\n      \"start\": { \"x\": 1, \"y\": 4, \"dir\": \"N\" },\n      \"hero\": \"💧\",\n      \"layouts\": [[\"...\", \".G.\", \"...\", \"...\", \"...\"]],\n      \"legend\": { \"G\": \"☁️\" },\n      \"goal\": { \"reach\": true }\n    },\n    \"palette\": {\n      \"actions\": [\n        { \"action\": \"forward\", \"label\": \"Maju\", \"icon\": \"⬆️\" },\n        { \"action\": \"turnLeft\", \"label\": \"Belok Kiri\", \"icon\": \"↩️\" },\n        { \"action\": \"turnRight\", \"label\": \"Belok Kanan\", \"icon\": \"↪️\" }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"optimalBlocks\": 3,\n    \"hints\": {\n      \"tier1\": [\n        \"Coba hitung, berapa kotak jarak Tetes Air ke awan? 🤔\",\n        \"Tetes Air menghadap ke mana sekarang? Apakah awannya ada di depannya?\"\n      ],\n      \"tier2\": [\n        \"Bayangkan kamu naik tangga ke kamar. Kamu melangkah satu, dua, tiga sampai di atas. Tetes Air juga begitu! 🪜\",\n        \"Seperti menghitung langkah ke pintu kelas, setiap blok Maju itu satu langkah.\"\n      ],\n      \"tier3\": [\n        \"Lihat lagi jumlah blok Maju-mu. Apakah sudah sama dengan jumlah kotak sampai awan?\",\n        \"Tetes Air sudah menghadap ke atas, jadi tidak perlu belok. Cukup atur berapa kali maju.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"solution\": [\n      { \"id\": \"s1\", \"kind\": \"action\", \"action\": \"forward\" },\n      { \"id\": \"s2\", \"kind\": \"action\", \"action\": \"forward\" },\n      { \"id\": \"s3\", \"kind\": \"action\", \"action\": \"forward\" }\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"L1-M2\",\n    \"level\": 1,\n    \"order\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Petualangan Siklus Air\",\n    \"subject\": \"IPA\",\n    \"concept\": \"sequencing\",\n    \"story\": \"Air di bumi selalu berkeliling tanpa henti. Susun perjalanan air dari laut sampai kembali ke laut!\",\n    \"goal\": \"Urutkan 4 tahap siklus air dengan benar.\",\n    \"world\": {\n      \"type\": \"sequence\",\n      \"cards\": [\n        { \"id\": \"evap\", \"label\": \"Air laut menguap\", \"emoji\": \"☀️\" },\n        { \"id\": \"cond\", \"label\": \"Uap mengembun jadi awan\", \"emoji\": \"☁️\" },\n        { \"id\": \"rain\", \"label\": \"Awan menurunkan